    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription.jpg" alt="Canggal inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription">Canggal inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canggal_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunawan Kartapranata, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Medang Kingdom, which its own inscriptions call Mataram and which this profile names as the register does, is first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription">Canggal inscription</a> of 732, found at the Gunung Wukir temple southwest of Magelang. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a>, it tells of a lingga raised on a hill in the Kunjarakunja area of Yawadwipa, a noble island rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and gold, on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjaya-of-mataram">Rakai Mataram Sang Ratu Sanjaya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> had been ruled by King Sanna, whose long reign was wise and virtuous; after his death the kingdom fell into disunity until Sanjaya, son of Sanna's sister Sannaha, took the throne, conquered the surrounding lands and brought peace. Sanjaya seems to have come to power about 717.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a>, a Sundanese text of the late 16th century, makes Sanjaya Sanna's son, tells how Sanna was defeated by Purbasora of Galuh and how Sanjaya avenged him and ruled West, Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>; romanticised as it is, its agreement with the Canggal inscription suggests a historical core. Mataram is Sanskrit for motherland; the name is first found in the Wuatan Tija inscription of 880, while Medang, perhaps from the medang hardwood tree, names the palace within it. Sanjaya was a Shaivite; his successor Panangkaran was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> Buddhist, a shift that divides historians between two rival dynasties, Sanjaya and Shailendra, and a single Shailendra house that changed its patronage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="Borobudur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pradaksina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heri nugroho, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mataram relied above all on agriculture, especially intensive wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation on the fertile volcanic soil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, which let the population grow and supplied the labour for the kingdom's public works. Most inscriptions record the grant of sima, taxed wet-rice land recognised by royal edict, often with part or all of its rice tax assigned to build and maintain a temple; a sima was made by opening forest or converting dry ladang to irrigated sawah, and the villages throughout the realm paid their taxes in rice. The Wonoboyo hoard of gold found in 1990, dated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a>'s reign, with gold coins shaped like corn seeds and stamped "ta" for tahil, shows an economy partly monetised.</p><p>The reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and Prambanan show soldiers, officials, court servants, travelling musicians and dancers, food and drink sellers, couriers, sailors, merchants, even thugs and robbers, beside the farmers who were the majority. Society recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> but applied them less rigidly than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>; Pigeaud divides it into rulers, religious authority, commoners and slaves, de Casparis into the court people, the priests and monks, and the commoners. In the East Javanese period the Waharu IV (931) and Garaman (1053) inscriptions record labour imported from Jenggi (Zanzibar), Pujut and Bondan to carry and pack crops for the ports; Naerssen thinks they came by purchase or as prisoners of war made slaves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat.jpg" alt="Nakhon Si Thammarat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nai_Mueang,_Mueang_Nakhon_Si_Thammarat_District,_Nakhon_Si_Thammarat_80000,_Thailand_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seksan Phonsuwan, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Shailendra kings were sea raiders as well as temple builders. In 767 Javanese and Kunlun ships raided Tonkin. In 774 Javanese or Kunlun vessels attacked Po Nagar at Nha Trang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, carried off the temple's linga and set fire to it; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> inscription there describes "men born in other lands, living on other foods, frightful to look at, unnaturally dark and lean, cruel as death, passing over the sea in ships". In 787 the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, "having come in vessels", burned the temple at Phan Rang. Dharanindra (r. 780–800), hailed in the Kelurak inscription of 782 as "the slayer of courageous enemies", took the Shailendras' power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Ligor</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and rose to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, by conquest or by kinship.</p><p>Sulaiman recorded in 851 a Javanese surprise attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, approaching the capital by river after a sea crossing, after which the young Khmer king was punished and his kingdom made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. At home the Shivagrha inscription of 856 records a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a> fought at a fortress on a hill protected by stone walls, won by his youngest son Dyah Lokapala. The Anjukladang inscription of 937 rewards a village for repelling invaders from Malayu, that is Srivijaya, and in 990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> launched a naval invasion to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>: Java's envoys of 992 told the Chinese court of continuous war, and Srivijaya had recovered by 1003.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="Mahabharata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king was the chakravartin, paramount ruler, governing the nagara or kadatwan from his puri, a walled palace of wooden pavilions. Administration had two levels, the court and the wanua villages. Between them stood officials titled Rakai or Samget, landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> who ruled a watak of several villages and passed the king's orders to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, the village heads. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a>, king from 10 May 898, centralised authority, restricted the aristocrats' autonomy, on 12 November 904 freed all Buddhist monasteries from tax, and in the Mantyasih (907) and Wanua Tengah III (908) inscriptions listed his predecessors back to Sanjaya, perhaps to legitimise an irregular accession.</p><p>Kingship changed hands uneasily. After Lokapala's death in 885 three kings followed in two years, and from 887 to 894 there was no single ruler until Jebang and then Balitung reunited the realm; Daksha, Balitung's prime minister, issued charters on his own authority before succeeding him around 910. Queens could rule: Pramodhawardhani stood beside Pikatan, and Isyana Tunggawijaya succeeded her father Sindok. Religion and state were intertwined. Sanjaya was Shaivite, Panangkaran through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaratungga">Samaratungga</a> Buddhist, Pikatan Shaivite again, yet Buddhist Plaosan, Banyunibo and Sajiwan rose in his reign, and brahmins and the sangha conducted the rituals of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> in 996.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mendut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mendut.jpg" alt="Mendut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candi_Mendut_-_facade_north_(2025)_-_img_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chainwit., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mataram era is hailed as the classical period of Javanese civilisation. Between Panangkaran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a> the Kedu and Kewu plains filled with temples: Kalasan and Sari (778), the 249-structure Manjusrigrha or Sewu complex (792), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawon">Pawon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> (completed about 825), Plaosan, and the Shivagrha of Prambanan, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple ever built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, raised by Pikatan in 856 and expanded through Lokapala and Balitung. Shailendra sculpture is counted among the finest Buddhist art in the world, and Borobudur's reliefs give the most complete rendering of the Karmavibhanga, Lalitavistara, Jataka, Avadana and Gandavyuha texts.</p><p>Javanese art spread with Javanese power. The Avalokiteshvaras of Bingin Jungut in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, Bidor in Perak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaiya">Chaiya</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> show Central Javanese influence long mislabelled Srivijayan, and the Bakong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> so resembles Borobudur that travellers or missions must have carried its design to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>. The Khmer prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-ii">Jayavarman II</a> spent years at the Shailendra court before returning around 790 to rule as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a> and proclaim independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in 802, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> era. The name Mdaη appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription">Laguna Copperplate Inscription</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, dated 900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaputradewa">Balaputra</a>, displaced in Java, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> inscription of 860 claimed the Shailendra inheritance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/airlangga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/airlangga.jpg" alt="Airlangga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airlangga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunawan Kartapranata, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Around 929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-sindok">Mpu Sindok</a> moved the centre of the kingdom from Central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, to Tamwlang and then Watugaluh near Jombang, founding the Isyana dynasty though closely related to the old house. Van Bemmelen blames a great eruption of Merapi in Wawa's reign, the Pralaya Mataram; Krom proposed an epidemic, Schrieke the burden of temple-building on peasants who drifted east; Coedès and de Casparis saw a retreat from the Buddhist Shailendras or from Srivijayan invasion, and de Casparis noted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> valley gave access to the north-coast ports and the Maluku spice trade which the isolated Kedu plain lacked.</p><p>In the late 10th century the rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> turned to war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a>, who had moved the capital to Wwatan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> and married his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendradatta">Mahendradatta</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayana-warmadewa">Udayana Warmadewa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, invaded Srivijaya by sea in 990 to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Sri Cudamani Warmadewa won Chinese support and repelled the invasion. In 1016–1017 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king of Wurawari, in the Banyumas area, struck from Lwaram during the wedding of Dharmawangsa's daughter, destroyed the palace and killed the king and most of the royal family. Javanese accounts call it the pralaya, the debacle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>, son of Udayana and Mahendradatta and the dead king's nephew, escaped to the Vanagiri forest, rallied support and in 1019 re-established the kingdom, including Bali, as Kahuripan, its capital near the Brantas estuary.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Medang Kingdom, the Mataram of its inscriptions, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom of Central and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> from Sanjaya's lingga of 732 to the pralaya of 1016–1017, whose Shailendra kings built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and Prambanan, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and whose last king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> died when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> Wurawari destroyed his palace.</em></p>

